  <Section id="motion" space="md" class="bg-subtle" reveal>
    <div class="gap-lg flex flex-col">
      <SectionHeader
        eyebrow="10 · motion"
        heading="Springs are the default, and they are free"
        lede="A spring is a curve, and linear() can carry an arbitrary curve — including the overshoot a cubic-bezier cannot express. So the entire spring character of this system costs zero JavaScript and runs on the compositor, which is why springs are the default here rather than a special occasion."
        split
      />

      <div class="gap-md grid md:grid-cols-2">
        <div class="bg-surface border-line p-md gap-sm flex flex-col rounded-md border">
          <p class="sg-meta">Hover either box. Same duration, different damping ratio.</p>
          <div class="gap-sm flex flex-col">
            <div class="sg-spring sg-spring-soft">
              <span class="sg-mono">ease-spring-soft · ζ 0.90 · 400ms</span>
            </div>
            <div class="sg-spring sg-spring-snappy">
              <span class="sg-mono">ease-spring-snappy · ζ 0.68 · 280ms · overshoots to 1.055</span>
            </div>
          </div>
          <p class="sg-meta">
            Generated by <span class="sg-mono">scripts/springs/</span> from stiffness/damping/mass, committed as
            CSS so a build never depends on the generator.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="bg-surface border-line p-md gap-sm flex flex-col rounded-md border">
          <h3 class="text-h6 text-primary">Scroll reveal — opt-in per Section</h3>
          <p class="text-sm text-secondary">
            This Section carries <span class="sg-mono">reveal</span>, which sets{' '}
            <span class="sg-mono">data-reveal-group</span> on its container. Default off: a page where everything
            animates in has no emphasis left to spend.
          </p>
          <p class="sg-meta">
            <strong class="text-primary">Dormant in this build.</strong> The native reveal module is supplied
            separately; the hook and the CSS contract are in place and wiring it is a drop-in. Nothing on this page
            carries <span class="sg-mono">data-reveal</span>, so nothing is hidden — a reveal system that hides
            content when its script does not run is a content-loss bug, not an animation.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="bg-surface border-line p-md gap-xs flex flex-col rounded-md border">
        <h3 class="text-h6 text-primary">The reduced-motion contract</h3>
        <ul class="list-bare gap-3xs text-sm text-secondary flex flex-col">
          <li>— reveals render their content <strong class="text-primary">visible, with no animation</strong> — never hidden;</li>
          <li>— transitions collapse to near-instant rather than being removed, so state changes still read as changes;</li>
          <li>— nothing autoplays;</li>
          <li>— only <code>transform</code> and <code>opacity</code> are ever animated, in either mode. They are the only properties the compositor can animate without layout or paint.</li>
        </ul>
        <p class="sg-meta">
          Turn on "Reduce motion" in the OS and reload: the spring boxes above snap, and nothing else on this page
          changes — which is the contract working.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </Section>

